import{_ as o,o as t,c as s,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Guard hooks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/hooks.md","filePath":"components/hooks.md"}'),i={name:"components/hooks.md"};function d(n,e,c,h,r,l){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="guard-hooks" tabindex="-1">Guard hooks <a class="header-anchor" href="#guard-hooks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Guard hooks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A hook is a program that the frontend runs at a fixed event, for example before a tool call or at the start of a session. The frontend sends the event as JSON on standard input. A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook runs before a tool call and can refuse it: exit status 2 refuses the call and shows the hook&#39;s standard error to the model, and an <code>ask</code> decision on standard output shows a prompt to the user. The hooks of this page use the Claude Code events <code>PreToolUse</code>, <code>PostToolUse</code>, <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>PermissionRequest</code>, <code>WorktreeCreate</code> and <code>WorktreeRemove</code>. <code>cc-status</code> also runs on the other events of a session.</p><p>The sources are in <code>hooks/</code>. <code>harness install --apply</code> copies them to <code>~/.claude/hooks/</code>. Claude Code calls them through the <code>hooks</code> section of its settings, which <code>harness install --apply</code> writes from <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>. On the <code>Bash</code> matcher, the directory and worktree tools and the Kaimon tools, Claude Code runs one program, the guard package <code>hooks/guard/</code>, which holds the checks of the guard scripts below. oh-my-pi calls seven scripts through its guard extension <code>adapters/omp/guards.ts</code>, OpenCode calls the same seven through its plugin <code>adapters/opencode/plugins/guards.ts</code>, and Antigravity calls them through its guard hook <code>adapters/antigravity/guard.py</code>. These are the five shell guards (<code>no-blind-stage.py</code>, <code>no-shell-file-write.py</code>, <code>gh-api-writes.py</code>, <code>rm-scope.py</code> and <code>git-archive-output.py</code>), the working-tree check of <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code>, and <code>kaimon-session.py</code>. Each of the three frontends runs its own copy of the scripts, which <code>harness install</code> writes to <code>hooks/</code> in its directory: <code>~/.omp/agent/hooks/</code>, <code>~/.config/opencode/hooks/</code> and <code>~/.gemini/config/hooks/</code>. Each hook still has one source. <code>hooks/probe.py</code> holds the test cases of the hooks, and <code>harness test</code> runs it.</p><p><code>harness install --apply</code> writes the command of each Python hook, in <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and in Antigravity&#39;s <code>hooks.json</code>, as the absolute path of the interpreter of the harness&#39;s Python environment, then the hook&#39;s path, each one shell word, quoted where the path holds a space. So a hook runs on the environment&#39;s Python, not on the <code>python3</code> that comes first on the <code>PATH</code>. Each Python hook keeps the shebang <code>#!/usr/bin/env python3</code>, for a run by hand such as the examples on this page. When the environment is missing, the command of a hook fails to start, with exit status 127. Claude Code reads an exit status other than 2 as a hook error that does not refuse the call, so under Claude Code such a hook fails open while the environment is missing. The guard&#39;s command ends in <code>|| exit 2</code>, so a guard that cannot start refuses the call. Antigravity reads a handler that prints nothing as a block.</p><p>The hooks prevent accidents. They are not a security boundary: each one reads the text of a command. <a href="./../security">The security model</a> says what they stop and what they do not stop. The page groups the hooks: first the guard package, then the guard scripts and <code>edit-scope.py</code>, then <code>worktree.py</code> and the session hooks, and last the probe.</p><h2 id="the-guard-package" tabindex="-1">The guard package <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-guard-package" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The guard package&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The guard is one program in <code>hooks/guard/</code> that decides a tool call before it runs. Claude Code runs it as a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on the matchers <code>Bash</code>, <code>mcp__ccd_directory__.*|EnterWorktree|ExitWorktree</code> and <code>mcp__kaimon__ex|mcp__kaimon__manage_repl</code>, with the command <code>&lt;python&gt; ~/.claude/hooks/guard --from claude || exit 2</code>. The <code>Bash</code> matcher runs <code>rtk hook claude</code> first, then the guard.</p><p>A table for each frontend, <code>frontends/claude.py</code> for Claude Code, turns the payload into a neutral call: the action, the command, the paths, the working directory and the caller. One module per check reads that call: the checks of the seven scripts <code>no-blind-stage.py</code>, <code>no-shell-file-write.py</code>, <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code>, <code>gh-api-writes.py</code>, <code>rm-scope.py</code>, <code>git-archive-output.py</code> and <code>kaimon-session.py</code>, and the directory check. Each check gives <code>deny</code>, <code>ask</code>, <code>allow</code> or <code>pass</code> with a reason. The guard answers the first of <code>deny</code>, <code>ask</code>, <code>allow</code> and <code>pass</code> that a check gives, with that check&#39;s reason: exit 2 with the reason on standard error for <code>deny</code>, a JSON decision for <code>ask</code> and <code>allow</code>, and no output for <code>pass</code>. A tool that the table does not name passes.</p><p>The directory check asks before a session leaves <code>~/Research</code>, or removes a worktree. A <code>change_directory</code> or <code>request_directory</code> call of the Claude desktop app, or an <code>EnterWorktree</code> call with a <code>path</code>, passes when the path resolves inside <code>~/Research</code>, after <code>~</code>, <code>..</code> and symbolic links. An <code>EnterWorktree</code> call with only a <code>name</code> passes, because <code>worktree.py</code> then decides the place. An <code>ExitWorktree</code> call passes only with <code>action: &quot;keep&quot;</code> and no <code>discard_changes</code>. Every other such call gets an <code>ask</code> decision.</p><p>The guard fails closed. It refuses a payload that is not UTF-8 or not a JSON object, or that has no <code>tool_name</code>; for a tool that it checks, a <code>tool_input</code> that is no object, a <code>Bash</code> call without a command, and a command of more than 1 MiB. A check that must resolve a path against the working directory refuses the command when the payload names none. A guard that raises refuses the call with the error. The checks read a command without <code>shlex</code>: a <code>#</code> inside a word, a backslash-newline and a quoted operator are read as the shell reads them, and a quoted operator or a joined line is also read the other way, so that neither hides a part of the command. <code>tests/guard/corpus.jsonl</code> holds the decision of each payload that the probe checks, and each difference from the scripts with its reason. A check of a shell command reads each path through <code>hooks/guard/paths.py</code>, which reads each directory and name once in each mode and asks each directory once whether it holds a <code>.git</code>; every check and every reading takes the walk of a word from one memo; the write check asks git once per work tree. A probe case finds no file-system read elsewhere in the package but a few named ones, which read once per process or one path of a directory call. The probe counts the reads of ten commands of 1 MiB (a long <code>cd</code> word, many <code>rm</code> operands, many <code>git init</code> outside a work tree), each at most two or three reads per distinct name of the command plus a constant, and the guard decides each of its timed commands of 1 MiB within 10 s. Test it from <code>hooks/</code> with the environment&#39;s interpreter, which is Python 3.14; the guard exits with status 2 on a <code>deny</code>:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;git add -nA&quot;},&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ~/.local/share/research-harness/python/bin/python</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> guard</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --from</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> claude</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="no-blind-stage-py" tabindex="-1"><code>no-blind-stage.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#no-blind-stage-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`no-blind-stage.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a <code>git add</code> that stages more than the paths it names. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run it on each shell call. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package instead.</p><p>It reads the command, splits it into segments, and finds each <code>git add</code>. It exits with status 2 on <code>-A</code>, <code>-u</code>, <code>--all</code>, <code>--update</code>, <code>--no-ignore-removal</code>, a short-flag cluster that holds <code>A</code> or <code>u</code>, and the pathspecs <code>.</code>, <code>./</code> and <code>:/</code>. The flag cluster is the reason for the hook: a deny glob cannot match <code>git add -nA</code> without also matching a file name that holds an <code>A</code>. The refusal tells the model to stage its paths by name.</p><p>It fails open: on an input that it cannot read, it exits with status 0. The deny list of the settings still refuses the common spellings. Test it without an install:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;git add -nA&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./no-blind-stage.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;exit </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">$?</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="no-shell-file-write-py" tabindex="-1"><code>no-shell-file-write.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#no-shell-file-write-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`no-shell-file-write.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a shell command that writes a file in a git working tree. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run it on each shell call. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package instead.</p><p>It exits with status 2 on a redirection such as <code>&gt;</code> or <code>&gt;&gt;</code>, on <code>tee</code>, on an in-place flag of <code>sed</code>, <code>perl</code> or <code>awk</code>, and on a <code>python3 -c</code> or a Python heredoc that opens a file to write. It resolves each target against the working directory and each <code>cd</code> of the command. A target under a scratch directory passes: <code>$TMPDIR</code>, <code>/tmp/</code>, <code>/private/tmp/</code>, <code>/var/folders/</code>, <code>/dev/</code> or <code>~/Research/.scratch/</code>. In a git working tree, every file is refused unless git ignores it. Outside one, only a file with a source suffix such as <code>.jl</code> or <code>.md</code> is refused. A quoted <code>&gt;</code> is text, not a redirection. The refusal tells the model to use the <code>Edit</code> and <code>Write</code> tools.</p><p>It fails open. It does not see a target that the shell computes, a Julia one-liner, or a writer inside a string argument such as <code>perl -e</code>. Test it without an install:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;echo x &gt; src/Foo.jl&quot;},&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/path/in/a/repo&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./no-shell-file-write.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="sandbox-semantics-py" tabindex="-1"><code>sandbox-semantics.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#sandbox-semantics-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`sandbox-semantics.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a command whose shape makes the Claude Code sandbox give a false result. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run its working-tree check, as <code>sandbox-semantics.py --work-tree</code>, on each shell call, and Antigravity&#39;s <code>guard.py</code> also imports its helpers to decide whether a command runs inside or outside the terminal sandbox.</p><p>The settings let a command run outside the sandbox only when every part of it starts with <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>ps</code> and no part redirects a file. A sandboxed <code>gh</code> cannot reach the network, and a sandboxed <code>git</code> cannot write <code>.git</code>. The failure then looks like a negative result or a certificate error. The hook exits with status 2 on four shapes:</p><ul><li>a <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>git</code> write inside <code>$( … )</code>, backticks or a loop;</li><li>a top-level <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>git</code> write in a command that is not exempt, for example after <code>cd other &amp;&amp;</code>, or a <code>gh</code> command that holds a backtick or <code>$(</code>;</li><li>a <code>$?</code> in a command that runs sandboxed;</li><li>a path argument of <code>git init</code>, <code>git clone</code>, <code>git worktree add</code>, <code>git worktree move</code> or <code>git bundle create</code> that starts with <code>/</code>, <code>~</code> or <code>$</code>, or holds <code>..</code>.</li></ul><p>It also exits with status 2 on a <code>git init</code> or <code>git clone</code> whose target is inside a git working tree. The hook follows each <code>cd</code> of the command to find the target. Under Claude Code a <code>cd</code> outside the agent&#39;s working directories is reset before the next call, so such a command runs in the agent&#39;s root and leaves a repository in another working tree.</p><p>Each refusal gives the form that works, for example a separate Bash call. It fails open. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;echo $(gh run view 1)&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./sandbox-semantics.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;exit </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">$?</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="gh-api-writes-py" tabindex="-1"><code>gh-api-writes.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#gh-api-writes-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`gh-api-writes.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook asks before a <code>gh api</code> write that is not a review or a comment, and refuses a remote delete. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run it on each shell call. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package instead.</p><p>It finds each top-level <code>gh api</code> and <code>glab api</code> call and determines the HTTP method in the same way as <code>gh</code>: <code>-X</code> or <code>--method</code>; otherwise POST when a body flag is present; otherwise GET. A GET, a HEAD and a <code>search/…</code> endpoint pass. The hook exits with status 2 on a DELETE from <code>gh api</code> or <code>glab api</code>. Any other <code>glab api</code> call passes. A review, an inline comment, a reply, and a comment on a pull request, an issue or a commit pass, so the review agents can post. A GraphQL query passes when it holds no mutation, or when each mutation is a review or comment operation. Every other write gets an <code>ask</code> decision, and the user sees a prompt. An edit of an existing comment also asks.</p><p>It fails open. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;gh api repos/o/r/hooks -f url=x&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./gh-api-writes.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="rm-scope-py" tabindex="-1"><code>rm-scope.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rm-scope-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rm-scope.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a recursive <code>rm</code> outside the scratch zones, and asks before a <code>git rm</code> or a <code>git merge</code> outside a worktree. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run it on each shell call. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package instead.</p><p>It follows the working directory through each <code>cd</code> and resolves each operand. A recursive <code>rm</code> passes when every operand is strictly below a worktree, <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, or strictly below <code>~/Research/.scratch/</code>. It exits with status 2 for a recursive <code>rm</code> of <code>/</code>, of the parent directory of the home directories, <code>/Users</code> or <code>/home</code>, or of any path below it. So a worktree itself goes through <code>git worktree remove</code>. A plain <code>rm</code>, and an operand that it cannot resolve, get no decision. A <code>git rm</code> gets an <code>ask</code> decision outside a worktree, with <code>-f</code> or <code>--force</code>, or with <code>-C</code>, <code>--git-dir</code> or <code>--work-tree</code>. A <code>git merge</code> in a worktree passes: the merge is local, and <code>git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD</code> undoes it. Outside a worktree, or with <code>-C</code>, <code>--git-dir</code> or <code>--work-tree</code>, it gets <code>ask</code>, except a <code>--ff-only</code> or <code>--no-edit</code> merge with none of <code>--no-ff</code>, <code>--squash</code>, <code>--no-commit</code> and <code>-m</code>. So the settings allow <code>git merge *</code>, and keep an <code>ask</code> only for <code>--abort</code>.</p><p>It fails closed to a prompt: a command that holds <code>rm</code> or <code>merge</code> and that it cannot read gets <code>ask</code>. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;rm -rf ../x&quot;},&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/Users/me/Research/Packages/A&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./rm-scope.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="edit-scope-py" tabindex="-1"><code>edit-scope.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#edit-scope-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`edit-scope.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook asks once per worktree and session before an edit of a git hook or a CI workflow: a path below <code>.githooks/</code> or <code>.github/workflows/</code> under <code>~/Research</code>. A git hook runs outside the sandbox at the next commit, and a workflow runs in CI with the repository&#39;s secrets when its branch is pushed. An <code>ask</code> on each such edit stops protecting anything once a person approves forty of them in a row.</p><p>Claude Code runs it on the <code>Edit</code>, <code>Write</code>, <code>MultiEdit</code> and <code>NotebookEdit</code> tools twice. As a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook it gets <code>ask</code> for such a path in a main checkout, and for one below a worktree, <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, unless this session holds a grant for that worktree. As a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook, <code>edit-scope.py --record</code>, it writes the grant after an edit there ran: only an approved edit runs, so only an approval makes a grant. The grants are in <code>~/.claude/logs/edit-scope.jsonl</code>, which the sandbox and an <code>Edit</code> deny keep from a session. The settings hold no <code>Edit</code> ask for these paths, because an <code>ask</code> rule outranks the hook&#39;s silence. OpenCode, oh-my-pi and Antigravity run no such hook and ask on each edit: their path lists take the two paths from <code>EDIT_SCOPE_ASKS</code> of <code>lib/harness/frontends.py</code>.</p><p>It fails closed to a prompt on a call that it cannot read. A hook that does not run lets every such edit through, because the settings hold no rule for them. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;session_id&quot;:&quot;s&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;file_path&quot;:&quot;&#39;&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$HOME</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&#39;/Research/Harness/.githooks/pre-push&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./edit-scope.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="git-archive-output-py" tabindex="-1"><code>git-archive-output.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#git-archive-output-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`git-archive-output.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a <code>git archive</code> that writes its file with <code>-o</code> or <code>--output</code>, and names the commands that read the tree of a commit without a permission prompt. oh-my-pi, OpenCode and Antigravity run it on each shell call. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package instead.</p><p>An agent writes the archive to read the unmodified tree of a commit, usually the base of its branch. <code>git</code> runs outside the sandbox, so <code>-o</code> can write to any path. For this reason the settings have <code>ask</code> rules on <code>-o</code>, and the user gets a prompt for what is only a read. The hook exits with status 2, and its message names other commands. <code>scripts/base-tree.jl</code> puts the tree, or some paths of it, into a new directory. <code>git show &lt;rev&gt;:&lt;path&gt;</code> gives one file, and <code>git ls-tree -r --name-only &lt;rev&gt;</code> gives the list of files. A tar file that is itself the result goes to the user.</p><p>It finds each top-level <code>git … archive</code> call, after global options such as <code>-C</code>, and reads the option in each spelling that git takes: <code>-o &lt;file&gt;</code>, <code>-o&lt;file&gt;</code>, behind other short options as in <code>-vo &lt;file&gt;</code>, <code>--output</code>, <code>--output=&lt;file&gt;</code>, and any prefix of <code>--output</code>. An argument after <code>--</code> is a path. A <code>git archive</code> to stdout passes.</p><p>It fails open. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;git archive -o base.tar origin/main&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./git-archive-output.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="kaimon-session-py" tabindex="-1"><code>kaimon-session.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#kaimon-session-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`kaimon-session.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook refuses a Kaimon call that names no session. oh-my-pi runs it on <code>mcp__kaimon_ex</code> and <code>mcp__kaimon_manage_repl</code>, OpenCode on <code>kaimon_ex</code> and <code>kaimon_manage_repl</code>, and Antigravity on <code>call_mcp_tool</code> calls to <code>kaimon</code> for <code>ex</code> and <code>manage_repl</code>. Claude Code runs its check in the guard package, on the matcher <code>mcp__kaimon__ex|mcp__kaimon__manage_repl</code>.</p><p>An <code>ex</code> call needs <code>ses</code>, and a <code>manage_repl</code> call needs <code>session</code>. Without the key, Kaimon uses the session that the agent is bound to, or otherwise the only one connected, which can be another agent&#39;s. <code>ex</code> ignores an unknown parameter, so the hook also exits with status 2 on an <code>ex</code> with <code>session</code> in place of <code>ses</code>. <code>check_eval</code> takes only the <code>eval_id</code> of its job, so the matcher leaves it out. The message names the rule of <code>skills/build-part/evidence.md</code>. It fails open. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;mcp__kaimon__ex&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;e&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./kaimon-session.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;exit </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">$?</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="worktree-py" tabindex="-1"><code>worktree.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#worktree-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`worktree.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook creates and removes the worktrees of Claude Code. Claude Code runs it on the events <code>WorktreeCreate</code> and <code>WorktreeRemove</code>. The reason is the sandbox: a <code>git worktree add</code> to a path below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code> runs sandboxed, where it cannot write <code>.git</code>.</p><p>On <code>WorktreeCreate</code> it fetches <code>origin</code> in the repository that holds <code>cwd</code>. It then creates <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;Repository&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code> on the branch <code>name</code>, from <code>origin/HEAD</code>, or from <code>origin/main</code> if <code>origin/HEAD</code> is not set. It prints the path on standard output. An existing branch is checked out, and an existing worktree of the repository is printed as it is. A name <code>agent-&lt;hex&gt;</code>, from a sub-agent, gets a detached worktree with no branch. The hook copies the main checkout&#39;s <code>Manifest.toml</code> into the new worktree when the manifest holds every package of the worktree&#39;s <code>[deps]</code> at a version inside its <code>[compat]</code>. Otherwise it copies none and says why on standard error, because Julia does not check <code>[compat]</code> at <code>using</code>, and a stale manifest loads old dependencies. On <code>WorktreeRemove</code> it runs <code>git worktree remove</code> without <code>--force</code>, so git refuses a worktree with changes, and the branch stays.</p><p>It fails closed: on each error it exits with status 1 and gives the reason, and it creates no worktree. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;hook_event_name&quot;:&quot;WorktreeCreate&quot;,&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;x&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./worktree.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;exit </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">$?</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="install-drift-py" tabindex="-1"><code>install-drift.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#install-drift-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`install-drift.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook warns when <code>~/.claude/</code> is behind the sources that <code>harness install</code> installs. Claude Code and Antigravity run it on the event <code>SessionStart</code>.</p><p><code>harness install --apply</code> writes the stamp <code>~/.claude/.harness-install.json</code>. The stamp holds the sources and a SHA-256 digest over the installed paths, their modes and the source bytes. The hook computes the same digest from the sources and compares the two. It warns when they differ, when the stamp is missing or cannot be read, and when a source cannot be read. The warning goes to the user and to the session, and it names the command to run. It does not run <code>harness install</code>, and an edit of the model tables does not count as drift.</p><p>It only warns: it blocks nothing and exits with status 0 for each input. <a href="./../daily-use#the-drift-warning-at-session-start">Typical use</a> says what to do when the warning appears.</p><h2 id="prompt-log-py" tabindex="-1"><code>prompt-log.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#prompt-log-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`prompt-log.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook writes one JSON line for each permission dialog to <code>~/.claude/logs/permission-requests.jsonl</code>. The environment variable <code>LOG</code> names another file. Claude Code runs it on the event <code>PermissionRequest</code>, for every tool, just before it shows the dialog.</p><p>Each line holds the time, the session, the working directory, the permission mode, the tool and its input. It cuts each string of the input to 400 characters, so a <code>Write</code> call does not log the whole file. The log shows which prompts a person still answers, by tool, by command and by mode. The transcript does not show this.</p><p>It decides nothing and prints nothing, so the dialog appears as it does without the hook. Every code path exits with status 0, also when the hook cannot read the input or write the log. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;ls&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> LOG</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$TMPDIR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/p.jsonl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./prompt-log.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="cc-status" tabindex="-1"><code>cc-status</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#cc-status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`cc-status`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This shell script passes each Claude Code hook event to the <code>cc-status</code> utility of iTerm2, which shows the state of the session in its tab. Claude Code runs it on the events <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>UserPromptSubmit</code>, <code>PreToolUse</code>, <code>PostToolUse</code>, <code>PermissionRequest</code>, <code>Notification</code>, <code>Stop</code>, <code>StopFailure</code>, <code>SubagentStop</code> and <code>SessionEnd</code>.</p><p>It hands its standard input and its arguments to <code>~/.config/iterm2/cc-status</code> unchanged. When that utility is not there, it exits with status 0 and does nothing. So one settings file serves a machine with iTerm2 and a machine without it. It guards nothing. <a href="./../dependencies#iterm2">Dependencies</a> says more about iTerm2.</p><h2 id="probe-py" tabindex="-1"><code>probe.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#probe-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`probe.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This script holds the test cases of the hooks and runs them. Its <code>GROUPS</code> split the cases into seven groups, and <code>harness test</code> runs each group as the part <code>probe:&lt;group&gt;</code>, so the probe needs no run of its own; <a href="./../development#tests">Development</a> describes the run.</p><p>A case is a hook, an input and the expected result. The probe runs each case in its own process, with a timeout, and prints one line per case: <code>ok</code> or <code>BAD</code>, the expected and the actual result. The last line gives the number of cases and the number of failed cases, and the exit status is 1 when any case fails. The group <code>commands</code> runs the guard package on each record of <code>tests/guard/corpus.jsonl</code> and on the cases that a line of it cannot hold: a command of 1 MiB and one byte more, commands of 1 MiB that the checks walk word by word, each held to 10 s with its seconds on its line, the count of the file-system reads, walk steps and <code>git</code> processes of such commands, a write target with a NUL, a <code>.git</code> that is a dangling link, a link to itself, a payload that is not UTF-8, the reason of the winning check, a frontend that the guard lacks, a guard that raises, the copy of <code>jsonio.py</code>, the file-system reads outside <code>paths.py</code>, and the rendered <code>Bash</code> entry with a guard that cannot start. The groups <code>omp</code>, <code>opencode</code> and <code>antigravity</code> run the seven guard scripts through those frontends. The probe also covers the other Python hooks of this page, the fail-closed cases of <code>worktree.py</code>, and the drift cases of <code>install-drift.py</code>. It also loads <code>adapters/omp/guards.ts</code> and the OpenCode plugins under <code>node</code>, so <code>node</code> must be on <code>PATH</code>. The hooks run against a fixture <code>HOME</code> in a temporary directory, never the real one. With no argument it runs every group; with group names, only those groups. Each run builds its own fixture:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">./probe.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [GROUP </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">...]</span></span></code></pre></div>',74)])])}const k=o(i,[["render",d]]);export{u as __pageData,k as default};
